\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Effect of density and magnetic field fluctuations on rf waves propagation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Yves Peysson, Lorenzo Morini and Joan Decker 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset toc
LatexCommand tableofcontents

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Low frequency fluctuations of the electron density or the magnetic field
 can strongly affect the propagation of rf waves in a magnetized plasma.
 This effect has been extensively investigated for the Lower Hybrid (LH)
 wave by solving a wave kinetic equation in the weak turbulence approximation
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ott78,bon82,vah92"

\end_inset

.
 In this approach, the flow of rf energy is described by a usual ray-tracing
 between random scattering events where the initial wave vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 can be modified both in amplitude and direction with the constraint to
 remain solution of the local dispersion relation.
 This physical mechanism is a good candidate for explaining a possible strong
 upshift of the power spectrum as the LH wave propagates in the plasma,
 even if the toroidal mode coupling for bridging the spectral gap is very
 weak.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
More recently, the possibility that edge density fluctuations could also
 modify significantly the localization of the current driven by the Electron
 Cyclotron (EC) wave has been investigated, an issue of great importance
 to the ITER design for the control of neoclassical tearing modes 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "tsi09"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Though primarily dedicated to the LH wave, the concept of wave scattering
 by low frequency density or magnetic field fluctuations can be easily extended
 to other types of rf waves provided 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{k}_{\Vert}\ll k_{\Vert}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{\omega}\ll\omega$
\end_inset

.
 Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\Vert}=\mathbf{k}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{b}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{k}_{\Vert}=\mathbf{\widetilde{k}}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{b}}$
\end_inset

 are the projections of the rf and fluctuation wave vectors respectively
 along the local direction of the magnetic field 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mathbf{b}}=\mathbf{B}/\left\Vert \mathbf{B}\right\Vert $
\end_inset

, while 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{\omega}$
\end_inset

 are the rf and fluctuation frequencies respectively.
 Consequently, the scattering process becomes a two dimensional problem
 in the plane perpendicular to the local magnetic field direction, a reasonable
 assumption regarding the large difference between the long wave length
 of the low frequency fluctuations of the plasma and the short length of
 the rf waves along 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mathbf{b}}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The parallel wave length 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{\Vert}$
\end_inset

 is related to the parallel refractive index 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\Vert}$
\end_inset

 of the rf wave by the simple relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{\Vert}=c/fN_{\Vert},$
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $f=\omega/2\pi$
\end_inset

 is the rf wave frequency.
 Using typical values corresponding to rf systems used on the Tore Supra
 tokamak 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pey01,len09"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{LH}=3.7GHz$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\Vert LH}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\simeq2$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{\Vert LH}$
\end_inset

 is about 
\begin_inset Formula $4cm$
\end_inset

 for the LH wave, while it is 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{\Vert EC}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\simeq1.3cm$
\end_inset

 for the Electron Cyclotron (EC) wave with 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $f_{EC}=110GHz$
\end_inset

 and 
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\noun default
\color inherit

\begin_inset Formula $N_{\Vert EC}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\simeq0.2$
\end_inset

.
 In both cases, the condition of validity of the model 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{\Vert}\ll\widetilde{\lambda}_{\Vert}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{\lambda}_{\Vert}\simeq qR_{p}$
\end_inset

 is well fullfiled.
 since the safety factor 
\begin_inset Formula $q>1$
\end_inset

 , and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{p}\sim m$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Calculations are organized in three steps.
 In the first section, the components of the scattered rf wave vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\mathbf{k}}$
\end_inset

 are calculated, according to the curvilinear coordinate system chosen for
 describing the magnetic equilibrium in C3PO 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pey08"

\end_inset

.
 The wave kinetic equation corresponding to the wave-wave interaction is
 then derived from first principles in the second section for any kind of
 rf waves and plasma model.
 Finally, in the third section, the wave kinetic equation is solved by a
 Monte-Carlo method and the procedure for the numerical implementation in
 the code is explicited.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Calculation of the scattered wave vector
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the rf wave vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 is scattered by low density fluctuations, it may change both in module
 and direction, with the condition
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{k}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{b}}=\check{\mathbf{k}}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{b}}=k_{\Vert}\label{eq:kparconservation}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none
while 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\mathbf{k}}$
\end_inset

 is still solution of the local dispersion relation.
 Consequently, the scattering process may be expressed in the simple form
 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{k}_{\bot}\cdot\check{\mathbf{k}}_{\bot}=k_{\bot}\check{k}_{\bot}\cos\beta,\label{eq:kperprotationbeta}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{k}_{\bot}=\left\Vert \check{\mathbf{k}}_{\bot}\right\Vert =\left\Vert \mathbf{\check{k}}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right\Vert $
\end_inset

 may change by wave-wave interaction if several modes of propagation are
 solutions of the dispersion relation for the same 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\Vert}$
\end_inset

, while 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is the angle of the rotation, between the initial and scattered wave vector
 directions in the plane perpendicular to 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mathbf{b}}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In Ref.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "bon82"

\end_inset

, the relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\left(\mathbf{k}_{\bot}\times\mathbf{\check{k}}\right)=k_{\bot}\check{k}_{\bot}\sin\beta$
\end_inset

 is also introduced.
 However, it is fully equivalent to (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:kperprotationbeta"

\end_inset

) and does not provide any additional information.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Unlike transitions are defined as those corresponding to 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{k}_{\bot}\neq k_{\bot}$
\end_inset

, while like ones to 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{k}_{\bot}=k_{\bot}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none
Using the coordinate system defined in Ref.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pey08"

\end_inset

, the components 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\check{k}_{\rho},\check{m},\check{n}\right)$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\mathbf{k}}$
\end_inset

 are thus fully determined by the components 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(k_{\rho},m,n\right)$
\end_inset

 of the wave vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 prior the scattering event and the two parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi=\check{k}_{\bot}/k_{\bot}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The conservation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:kparconservation"

\end_inset

) of the parallel component of the wave vector in the scattering process
 may be expressed in the form,
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\sigma_{I}P\dfrac{\check{m}}{r}\cos\alpha+\sigma_{B}T\dfrac{\check{n}}{R}=\sigma_{I}P\dfrac{m}{r}\cos\alpha+\sigma_{B}T\dfrac{n}{R}\label{eq:ws1}\end{equation}

\end_inset

which gives the first equation which must be satisfied by components of
 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\check{\mathbf{k}}$
\end_inset

 defined as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\check{\mathbf{k}}=\check{k}_{\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert \widehat{\mathbf{\rho}}+\dfrac{\check{m}}{r}\widehat{\mathbf{\theta}}+\dfrac{\check{n}}{R}\widehat{\mathbf{\phi}}\label{eq:kcovariant}\end{equation}

\end_inset

according to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pey08"

\end_inset

.
 Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mathbf{b}}=\sigma_{B}T\mathbf{\widehat{\phi}}+\sigma_{I}P\mathbf{\hat{s}}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $T=B_{T}/B$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P=B_{P}/B$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{B}$
\end_inset

 is the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\phi}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{I}$
\end_inset

 is the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{\phi}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{T}=\left\vert I\left(\psi\right)\right\vert /R$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{P}=\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert /R$
\end_inset

 are definite positive and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\hat{s}}=\mathbf{\widehat{\phi}}\times\mathbf{\widehat{\rho}}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\nabla}\rho=\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert \mathbf{\mathbf{\widehat{\rho}}}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\hat{s}}$
\end_inset

 is orientated counter-clockwise in the poloidal plane.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Expressing the scalar product 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}_{\bot}\cdot\check{\mathbf{k}}_{\bot}$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left(\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\cdot\left(\mathbf{\check{k}}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)=\left(\mathbf{k}\cdot\check{\mathbf{k}}\right)\left(\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)-\left(\mathbf{k}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\left(\mathbf{\widehat{\mathbf{b}}}\cdot\check{\mathbf{k}}\right)=\mathbf{k}\cdot\check{\mathbf{k}}-k_{\Vert}^{2}\label{eq:}\end{equation}

\end_inset

and using (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:kcovariant"

\end_inset

), (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:kperprotationbeta"

\end_inset

) becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
k_{\bot}\check{k}_{\bot}\cos\beta=k_{\bot}^{2}\chi\cos\beta=k_{\rho}\check{k}_{\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert ^{2}+\dfrac{m\check{m}}{r^{2}}+\dfrac{n\check{n}}{R^{2}}-\frac{\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert \sin\alpha}{r}\left(m\check{k}_{\rho}+\check{m}k_{\rho}\right)-k_{\Vert}^{2}\label{eq:ws2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

since 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\hat{\rho}}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{\theta}}=-\sin\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\hat{\theta}}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{\phi}}=\mathbf{\hat{\rho}}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{\phi}}=0$
\end_inset

 
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\noun default
\color inherit

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pey08"

\end_inset


\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none
.
 This the second equation between components of 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\mathbf{k}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\check{k}_{\bot}$
\end_inset

 is determined from the dispersion relation from which 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{k}_{\bot}$
\end_inset

 is evaluated, the third equation is simply
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\noun default
\color inherit

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\chi^{2}k_{\bot}^{2}=\check{k}_{\rho}^{2}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert ^{2}-2\check{k}_{\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert \dfrac{\check{m}\sin\alpha}{r}+\dfrac{\check{m}^{2}}{r^{2}}+\dfrac{\check{n}^{2}}{R^{2}}-k_{\Vert}^{2}\label{ws3}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The set of equations (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:ws1"

\end_inset

), (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:ws2"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "ws3"

\end_inset

) gives an implicit formulation of the components of 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\mathbf{k}}$
\end_inset

 as a function of 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none
those of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

.
 
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\noun default
\color inherit
However, even if 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{k}_{\rho}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{m}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{n}$
\end_inset

 can be fully defined in this way, this formulation is not appropriate for
 fast numerical calculations, owing to the non-linearity of (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "ws3"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A more suitable approach for an efficient numerical implementation consists
 in deriving an explicit formulation of the components of 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\mathbf{k}}$
\end_inset

 as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi$
\end_inset

, considering the geometrical transformation from 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\mathbf{k}}$
\end_inset

.
 Let define the orthonormal frame of reference 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\left(\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}/k_{\bot},\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}/k_{\bot},\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)$
\end_inset

 formed by the local directions 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mathbf{b}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 of the magnetic field and wave vector respectively.
 The scattered wave vector may be expressed 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{multline}
\check{\mathbf{k}}=\left(\check{\mathbf{k}}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\widehat{\mathbf{b}}+\left(\check{\mathbf{k}}\cdot\frac{\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}}{k_{\bot}}\right)\frac{\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}}{k_{\bot}}+\left(\check{\mathbf{k}}\cdot\left[\frac{\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}}{k_{\bot}}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right]\right)\left[\frac{\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}}{k_{\bot}}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right]\\
=\check{k}_{\Vert}\widehat{\mathbf{b}}+\check{k}_{\bot}\sin\beta\frac{\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}}{k_{\bot}}-\check{k}_{\bot}\cos\beta\frac{\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}}{k_{\bot}}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\\
=\check{k}_{\Vert}\widehat{\mathbf{b}}+\chi\left(\sin\beta\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}-\cos\beta\left(\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\label{eq:khat}\end{multline}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\check{k}_{\bot}=\check{\mathbf{k}}\cdot\frac{\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}}{k_{\bot}}\label{eq:khatperp}\end{equation}

\end_inset

and it can be easily verified that for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi=1,$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\mathbf{k}}=\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 is well recovered, since 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{k}_{\Vert}=k_{\Vert}$
\end_inset

 in the model.
 From (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:khat"

\end_inset

), the second right handside term corresponds to a rotation of an angle
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 combined with an homothetic transformation of the module of the perpendicular
 component of the wave vector.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The scattering operator 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}_{\beta}^{\chi}\left(\mathbf{k}\right)$
\end_inset

 is then
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}_{\beta}^{\chi}\left(\mathbf{k}\right)=\left(\mathbf{k}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\widehat{\mathbf{b}}+\chi\left(\sin\beta\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}-\cos\beta\left(\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\label{eq:scatteringoperator}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\noun default
\color inherit
and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}_{0}^{1}=\mathcal{I}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}$
\end_inset

 is the identity 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none
transformation
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\noun default
\color inherit
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:kcovariant"

\end_inset

),
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\check{k}_{\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert -\dfrac{\check{m}}{r}\sin\alpha & =k_{\Vert}\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\rho}+\chi\sin\beta\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\rho}-\chi\cos\beta\left(\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\rho}\label{eq:kmhatrho}\\
-\check{k}_{\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert \sin\alpha+\dfrac{\check{m}}{r} & =k_{\Vert}\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\theta}+\chi\sin\beta\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\theta}-\chi\cos\beta\left(\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\theta}\label{eq:kmhattheta}\\
\dfrac{\check{n}}{R} & =k_{\Vert}\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\phi}+\chi\sin\beta\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\phi}-\chi\cos\beta\left(\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\phi}\label{eq:khatphi}\end{align}

\end_inset

where all scalar products must be evaluated using the definitions of 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\rho}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\phi}$
\end_inset

 introduced in Ref.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pey08"

\end_inset

, taking in particular account that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\hat{\rho}}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{\theta}}=-\sin\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By combining (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:kmhatrho"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:kmhattheta"

\end_inset

),
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{multline}
\check{k}_{\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert \cos^{2}\alpha=k_{\Vert}\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\rho}+\chi\sin\beta\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\rho}-\chi\cos\beta\left(\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\rho}\\
+\left(k_{\Vert}\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\theta}+\chi\sin\beta\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\theta}-\chi\cos\beta\left(\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\theta}\right)\sin\alpha\label{eq:khatrho}\end{multline}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula \begin{multline}
\dfrac{\check{m}}{r}\cos^{2}\alpha=\left(k_{\Vert}\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\rho}+\chi\sin\beta\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\rho}-\chi\cos\beta\left(\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\rho}\right)\sin\alpha\\
+k_{\Vert}\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\theta}+\chi\sin\beta\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\theta}-\chi\cos\beta\left(\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\theta}\label{eq:khattheta}\end{multline}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Evaluating 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\check{k}_{\rho},\check{m},\check{n}\right)$
\end_inset

 requires to calculate all scalar products of the forms 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\ldots$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\ldots$
\end_inset

 and
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\mathbf{k}\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\right)\times\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\ldots$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\ldots=\widehat{\rho},\widehat{\theta},\mathbf{\widehat{\phi}}$
\end_inset

.

\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\noun default
\color inherit
 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\rho} & =0\label{eq:bdotrho}\\
\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\theta} & =\sigma_{I}P\cos\alpha\label{eq:bdottheta}\\
\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\cdot\widehat{\phi} & =\sigma_{B}T\label{eq:bdotphi}\end{align}

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\widehat{\phi}}\cdot\widehat{\rho}=\hat{\mathbf{s}}\cdot\widehat{\rho}=\mathbf{\widehat{\phi}}\cdot\widehat{\theta}=\hat{\mathbf{s}}\cdot\mathbf{\widehat{\phi}}=0$
\end_inset

 and using the relation 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\hat{s}}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{\theta}}=\cos\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 Using the vectorial transformations
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\noun default
\color inherit
 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
